{"name":"Tuna","hex":"#46494e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Tuna","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Various towns and  former settlements in Sweden."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A god, considered the son of Manga-wai-roa and source of eels."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Tuna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tuna","blocks":[{"text":"Tuna are saltwater fish belonging to the tribe Thunnini, a subgrouping of the mackerel family (Scombridae). The Thunnini comprise 15 species across five genera, the sizes of which vary from the bullet tuna to the Atlantic bluefin tuna, which averages 2 m (6.6 ft) and is believed to live up to 50 years."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Tuna_assortment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuna","wikidata":"Q6146274"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg/500px-Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg/1280px-Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg/500px-Tuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relative sizes of various tunas, with the Atlantic bluefin tuna (top) at about 8 ft (2.4 m) in this sample","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATuna_Relative_Sizes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Thunnus_alalunga_Ford.jpg/500px-Thunnus_alalunga_Ford.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Thunnus_alalunga_Ford.jpg/1280px-Thunnu